2026-06-30 15:13:33,992 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-51f3f6d8-3d6c-4ffb-a19f-5f844b626b6f', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: disease\nsdrf_column: characteristics[disease]\nontology: EFO:0000408, MONDO, DOID, PATO\ndescription: Disease state of the sample.\ninstructions: For healthy/normal samples use "normal" (PATO:0000461). For diseased\n  samples use MONDO or DOID terms. Return null only if completely unstated.\n  Most XL-MS samples are "normal" unless explicitly studying a disease model.\n\nterm: phenotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phenotype]\nontology: EFO:0000651, PATO, EFO\ndescription: Observable characteristics or traits (drug sensitivity, molecular markers,\n  expression phenotypes, environmental responses).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. This captures how the sample behaves/appears\n  (distinct from disease diagnosis).\n\nterm: treatment\nsdrf_column: characteristics[treatment]\nontology: EFO:0000727, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Treatment or perturbation applied to the sample (drug, stimulus,\n  environmental stress, growth condition change).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable. Examples: "doxycycline\n  treatment", "heat shock", "DMSO vehicle control".\n\nterm: compound\nsdrf_column: characteristics[compound]\nontology: CHEBI:24431, CHEBI, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Chemical compound or drug applied to the sample.\ninstructions: Use ChEBI if possible. Return null if no compound treatment. Do NOT\n  include the crosslinking reagent here — that goes in comment[cross-linker].\n\nterm: dose\nsdrf_column: characteristics[dose]\nontology: NCIT:C25488\ndescription: Dose or concentration of compound treatment.\npattern: number + unit (e.g. 10 mg/kg, 50 uM, 100 nM, 10 mg/mL)\ninstructions: Return null if no compound treatment or dose not stated.\n\nterm: genetic modification\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genetic modification]\nontology: EFO:0000510, EFO\ndescription: Method of genetic modification (knockout, knockdown, overexpression,\n  transduction). Describes HOW the modification was done.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: perturbation\nsdrf_column: characteristics[perturbation]\nontology: PRIDE:0000700\ndescription: Type of perturbation applied (environmental/experimental perturbation).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: depletion\nsdrf_column: characteristics[depletion]\nontology: PRIDE:0000020\ndescription: Whether abundant protein depletion was performed.\nallowed_values: no depletion, depletion, not applicable\ninstructions: Return "no depletion" if not mentioned. Return "depletion" if\n  high-abundance protein removal is described (e.g. albumin depletion).\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nIdentification of Plk1 interacting proteins by XChIP-MS\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nPolo-like kinase 1 (Plk1) is an important protein kinase for checkpoint recovery and adaptation in response to DNA damage and replication stress. However, although Plk1 is present in S phase, little is known about its localization and function during unperturbed DNA replication. Using Xenopus laevis egg extracts, mimicking early embryonic replication, we developed a Plk1 chromatin immunoprecipitation approach followed by a proteomic shotgun analysis (XChIP-MS) to identify Plk1 interactions with chromatin and with specific nuclear proteins. We purified and cross-linked nuclei after pre-RC assembly before the start of DNA replication, or later during DNA replication. The DNA was fractionated by sonication and immunoprecipited using anti Plk1 or control antibodies then proteins were analyzed by nanoLC/MS/MS.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor Chromatin immunoprecipitation coupled to Mass Spectrometry (XChIP-MS), sperm nuclei (4000 nuclei/µl) were incubated in fresh extracts and allowed to replicate for 15 and 60 minutes. Reactions were stopped by a 1/5 dilution in nuclear isolation buffer (NIBS) (50 mM Hepes, pH 7.5, 150 mM NaCl, 2 mM MgCl2, 10 % sucrose). Nuclei were pelleted through 150 µl of a 20 % sucrose cushion in NIBS buffer for 5 min at 5 000 g (4°C). The purification was repeated once and the pellet resuspended in 100 µl ChIP buffer (50 mM Hepes, pH 7.5, 150 mM NaCl, 1 mM EDTA). Crosslinking was performed using 1% formaldehyde for 8 min, reaction was quenched by 250 mM Glycine and nuclei were pelleted for 5 min at 5 000 g (4°C), then lysed in 200 µl ChIP buffer containing 0.5% triton X100 (ChIP lysis buffer). The DNA was sheared into fragments of 100-300 bp using Bioruptor Plus sonicator in 1.5ml TPX tubes; 30 cycles 30sec/30sec ON/OFF (high power) then the soluble DNA was clarified by centrifugation for 15 min at 19 000 g (4°). Sample was divided threefold; One third was reversed cross-linked in 50 mM Tris pH 8 10 mM EDTA 1% SDS at 65°C overnight, treated with 1µg/µl RNAse A for 1h at 37°C, then with 1µg/µl proteinase K for 2h at 55°C, extracted with phenol-chloroform, ethanol precipitated for DNA shredding control on agarose gel electrophoresis. The two other thirds were diluted two-fold and incubated overnight at 4°C with either anti-Plk1 or control pre-immune serum DMP-crosslinked cross-linked to protein A Sepharose. Beads were washed twice with 200 µl ChIP lysis buffer, once in 200 µl ChIP buffer containing 500 mM NaCl and then once in 200 µl ChIP buffer. Finally, the beads were resuspended in 20 μl 2X Laemmli loading buffer. Proteins eluted from the immune complexes were concentrated in a short migration on a SDS-PAGE gel and visualized by Coomassie Blue staining. The bands containing the whole sample were cut and subjected to in-gel trypsin digestion using standard conditions including reduction and alkylation. After overnight tryptic digestion, peptides were extracted with 60% acetonitrile and 0.1% (v/v) formic acid. Tryptic peptides were vacuum dried and resuspended in 0.1% (v/v) formic acid prior to nanoLC-MS/MS mass spectrometry analyses. Tryptic peptides from the four conditions were analysed separately by TripleTOF 4600 mass spectrometer (ABSciex) coupled to the nanoRSLC system (Thermo Fisher Scientific) equipped with a trap column (Acclaim PepMap100C18, 75 µm i.d.×2 cm, 3µm) and an analytical column (Acclaim PepMapRSLCC18, 75 µm i.d.× 75 cm, 2µm, 100 Å). Peptides were loaded at 5 µl.min-1 with 0.05% TFA in 5% acetonitrile and peptides separation was performed at a flow rate of 300nl.min-1 with a 5 to 35% solvent B gradient in 180 min. Solvent A was 0.1% formic acid in water, and solvent B was 0.1% formic acid in 100% acetonitrile. MS/MS spectra were acquired by a data-dependent acquisition method involving selection of the 20 precursors giving the most intense signals, CID fragmentation with the Q1 quadrupole set at low resolution to improve sensitivity.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
